  "summary": "The Markup/CalMatters reports on covert license plate readers installed by federal agencies like Border Patrol and DEA along Southern California highways via Caltrans permits, with EFF mapping over 40 hidden cameras. Privacy advocates argue the federal surveillance bypasses California's ALPR law, and local agencies continue sharing ALPR data with federal immigration authorities in violation of state law.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "By allowing Border Patrol and the DEA to put license plate readers along the border, they're essentially bypassing the protections under (California law). That is a backdoor around it.",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "Caltrans does not operate, manage, or determine the specific use of technology or equipment installed by permit holders, nor does it have access to any of the collected data",
      "paragraph_idx": 28
    },
    {
      "quote": "The San Pablo Police Department in Northern California, one of the law enforcement agencies that received letters from the attorney general's office, shared license plate data with the Border Patrol as recently as last month",
      "paragraph_idx": 33
    }
  ],
